# 1. Recommended default

For a new Python application or package, use:

```
src/ layout
pyproject.toml
uv
uv.lock
Hatchling build backend
pytest
Ruff
Pyright
CI
```

`pyproject.toml` centralizes project metadata, dependencies, build configuration, and tool settings. The Packaging User Guide defines `[build-system]`, `[project]`, and `[tool]` as its main configuration areas.[[packaging.python](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/guides/writing-pyproject-toml/)]

# 2. Project structure

```
trustdesk/
├── pyproject.toml
├── README.md
├── LICENSE
├── uv.lock
├── src/
│   └── trustdesk/
│       ├── __init__.py
│       ├── __main__.py
│       ├── cli.py
│       ├── config.py
│       ├── domain/
│       │   ├── tickets.py
│       │   └── users.py
│       ├── application/
│       │   └── ticket_service.py
│       ├── infrastructure/
│       │   ├── database.py
│       │   └── llm_clients.py
│       └── interfaces/
│           ├── repositories.py
│           └── providers.py
└── tests/
    ├── conftest.py
    ├── test_tickets.py
    └── test_services.py
```

Responsibilities:

```
domain/          → business concepts and rules
application/     → use cases and services
infrastructure/ → databases, APIs, files, external systems
interfaces/      → Protocols and abstract contracts
cli.py           → command-line interface
config.py        → configuration loading
tests/           → automated tests
```

The `src/` layout helps ensure tests import the installed package rather than accidentally importing files directly from the project root.

# 3. `pyproject.toml`

A practical starting point:

```
[build-system]
requires = ["hatchling"]
build-backend = "hatchling.build"

[project]
name = "trustdesk"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "AI-assisted support operations."
readme = "README.md"
requires-python = ">=3.12"
license = { file = "LICENSE" }
authors = [
    { name = "Toussaint Jacobs" },
]
dependencies = [
    "httpx>=0.27",
]

[project.optional-dependencies]
postgres = [
    "psycopg[binary]>=3.2",
]

[dependency-groups]
dev = [
    "pytest>=8",
    "pytest-cov>=5",
    "ruff>=0.6",
    "pyright>=1.1",
]

[project.scripts]
trustdesk = "trustdesk.cli:main"

[tool.ruff]
line-length = 79
target-version = "py312"

[tool.ruff.lint]
select = ["E", "F", "I", "B", "UP"]

[tool.pyright]
include = ["src", "tests"]
typeCheckingMode = "standard"
pythonVersion = "3.12"

[tool.pytest.ini_options]
testpaths = ["tests"]
addopts = "-ra"
```

The fields have different purposes:

```
[build-system] → how the package is built
[project]      → package metadata and runtime dependencies
[dependency-groups] → development dependencies
[project.scripts]   → installed CLI commands
[tool.*]        → Ruff, Pyright, pytest, and other tools
```

# 4. uv workflow

Create a project:

```
uv init trustdesk
cd trustdesk
```

Add runtime dependencies:

```
uv add httpx
```

Add development dependencies:

```
uv add --dev pytest pytest-cov ruff pyright
```

Synchronize the environment:

```
uv sync
```

Run commands inside the managed environment:

```
uv run pytest
uv run ruff check .
uv run pyright
```

Run your application:

```
uv run python -m trustdesk
```

Create a console command using `[project.scripts]`:

```
[project.scripts]
trustdesk = "trustdesk.cli:main"
```

Then run:

```
uv run trustdesk
```

`uv` manages project dependencies and records exact resolutions in `uv.lock`; the official workflow includes `uv add`, `uv sync`, `uv run`, and building publishable distributions.[[docs.astral](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/projects/dependencies/)][[docs.astral](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/guides/projects/)]

## Commit these

```
pyproject.toml
uv.lock
```

## Do not commit these

```
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
```

# 5. Virtual environments

Using standard Python:

```
python -m venv .venv
```

Activate on Linux/macOS:

```
source .venv/bin/activate
```

Activate on Windows PowerShell:

```
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

Install your project:

```
python -m pip install -e .
```

Using uv, you usually do not need to activate the environment manually:

```
uv sync
uv run python app.py
```

The environment prevents conflicts between projects:

```
TrustDesk → httpx version A
Burvex    → httpx version B
```

Each project can have its own dependency resolution.

# 6. uv, Hatch, and Poetry

|Tool|Best known for|Use it when|
|---|---|---|
|uv|Fast environments, dependencies, lockfiles, Python versions|Strong default for new projects|
|Hatch/Hatchling|Build backend and environment management|Publishing libraries or using Hatch workflows|
|Poetry|Integrated dependency and project management|Your team already standardizes on Poetry|
|pip + venv|Minimal standard workflow|Small scripts or maximum simplicity|

You do not need to use all of them.

A clean combination is:

```
uv          → dependency and environment management
Hatchling   → package build backend
pyproject   → metadata and tool configuration
```

Hatchling does not prevent you from using uv. One manages building; the other manages the development environment and dependencies.

# 7. Building packages

Build a wheel and source distribution:

```
uv build
```

or:

```
python -m build
```

The result is usually:

```
dist/
├── trustdesk-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl
└── trustdesk-0.1.0.tar.gz
```

A wheel is a ready-to-install built distribution.

A source distribution contains source code and build metadata.

Test the wheel in a clean environment:

```
python -m venv /tmp/test-env
source /tmp/test-env/bin/activate

python -m pip install dist/*.whl
python -c "import trustdesk"
```

This catches errors that local development may hide:

- Missing package files.
- Broken imports.
- Incorrect metadata.
- Missing dependencies.
- Incorrect console scripts.
- Improper package discovery.

The Python Packaging User Guide recommends building distribution artifacts and testing installation before publishing.[[packaging.python](https://packaging.python.org/tutorials/packaging-projects/)]

# 8. Publishing

Install Twine:

```
python -m pip install twine
```

Upload to TestPyPI first:

```
python -m twine upload \
    --repository testpypi \
    dist/*
```

Then test installation:

```
python -m pip install \
    --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ \
    trustdesk
```

After verification:

```
python -m twine upload dist/*
```

Use:

- API tokens.
- CI secret storage.
- Trusted publishing.
- Environment variables.

Never put package-index credentials in:

```
source code
pyproject.toml
README.md
Git commits
```

Do not overwrite an existing published version. Release a new version:

```
0.1.0 → 0.1.1
```

# 9. Dependency management at scale

Separate dependencies into categories.

## Runtime dependencies

```
[project]
dependencies = [
    "httpx>=0.27,<1.0",
]
```

Required when users install and run your package.

## Development dependencies

```
[dependency-groups]
dev = [
    "pytest",
    "ruff",
    "pyright",
]
```

Needed for development and CI, but not for normal package users.

## Optional feature dependencies

```
[project.optional-dependencies]
postgres = [
    "psycopg[binary]>=3.2",
]
```

Users install:

```
pip install trustdesk[postgres]
```

Applications should normally commit a lockfile:

```
uv.lock
```

Libraries should generally declare compatible dependency ranges rather than forcing one exact version unless necessary.

A useful distinction:

```
Application → lock exact versions
Library     → publish compatible version ranges
```

# 10. Configuration and secrets

Do not hardcode:

```
API_KEY = "secret"
```

Use environment variables:

```
import os


api_key = os.environ["TRUSTDESK_API_KEY"]
```

Typed settings object:

```
from dataclasses import dataclass
import os


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Settings:
    model_name: str
    debug: bool


def load_settings() -> Settings:
    return Settings(
        model_name=os.getenv(
            "MODEL_NAME",
            "default-model",
        ),
        debug=os.getenv(
            "DEBUG",
            "false",
        ).lower() == "true",
    )
```

Keep configuration loading separate from business logic.

# 11. Application architecture

A useful dependency direction is:

```
CLI/API
  ↓
application services
  ↓
domain objects and rules
  ↓
interfaces/protocols
  ↑
infrastructure implementations
```

Example protocol:

```
from typing import Protocol


class TicketRepository(Protocol):
    def save(self, ticket: dict) -> None:
        ...

    def get_by_id(
        self,
        ticket_id: str,
    ) -> dict | None:
        ...
```

Application service:

```
class TicketService:
    def __init__(
        self,
        repository: TicketRepository,
    ):
        self._repository = repository

    def create_ticket(
        self,
        ticket: dict,
    ) -> dict:
        self._repository.save(ticket)
        return ticket
```

Infrastructure implementation:

```
class InMemoryTicketRepository:
    def __init__(self):
        self._tickets = {}

    def save(self, ticket):
        self._tickets[ticket["id"]] = ticket

    def get_by_id(self, ticket_id):
        return self._tickets.get(ticket_id)
```

The application service does not depend directly on PostgreSQL, Redis, or a cloud API.

# 12. Professional command sequence

For a new project:

```
uv init trustdesk
cd trustdesk

uv add httpx
uv add --dev pytest pytest-cov ruff pyright

uv sync

uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format .
uv run pyright
uv run pytest --cov=src

uv build
```

For CI:

```
uv sync --locked
uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format --check .
uv run pyright
uv run pytest --cov=src
```

The `--locked` behavior helps ensure CI uses the committed lockfile rather than silently resolving a different environment.

# 13. Mistakes to avoid

- Put code in `src/`, not randomly throughout the project root.
- Do not commit `.venv`.
- Do commit the lockfile for applications.
- Do not publish without testing the wheel.
- Do not hardcode secrets.
- Do not mix package managers carelessly.
- Do not add multiple packages before the project needs them.
- Do not expose internal modules as public API accidentally.
- Do not use editable installs as your production deployment artifact.
- Do not publish a new build under an already-used version.

# 14. Final recommendation

For your current projects, use:

```
src/ layout
pyproject.toml
uv
uv.lock
Hatchling
pytest
Ruff
Pyright
```

Recommended development loop:

```
uv add dependency
uv sync
uv run pytest
uv run ruff check .
uv run pyright
uv build
```

The main principle is:

> Use `pyproject.toml` to describe the project, uv to reproduce the environment, a build backend to create artifacts, and clean package boundaries to keep the application maintainable.

For TrustDesk or Burvex, begin with one well-structured package. Move to a workspace or multiple independently published packages only when shared libraries, independent releases, or team boundaries justify the added complexity.[[packaging.python](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/guides/writing-pyproject-toml/)][[docs.astral](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/projects/dependencies/)][[docs.astral](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/guides/projects/)][[packaging.python](https://packaging.python.org/tutorials/packaging-projects/)]
